1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,300
How do professionals get better at what they do?

2
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:06,000
How do they get great?

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:07,500
And there are two views about this.

4
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:09,100
One is the traditional view.

5
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:14,300
That is that you go to school, you study, you practice, you learn, you graduate.

6
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:17,600
And then you go out into the world and you make your way on your own.

7
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:18,900
That's how doctors learn.

8
00:00:18,900 --> 00:00:21,500
That's how lawyers do, scientists.

9
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,800
Now, the contrasting view comes out of sports.

10
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:27,900
And they say, you are never done.

11
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:29,600
Everybody needs a coach.

12
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:33,300
The greatest in the world needs a coach.

13
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:35,700
So I tried to think about this as a surgeon.

14
00:00:38,076 --> 00:00:44,476
pay someone to come into my operating room, observe me, and critique me.

15
00:00:45,076 --> 00:00:45,976
It seems absurd.

16
00:00:47,476 --> 00:00:49,976
Expertise means not needing to be coached.

17
00:00:50,676 --> 00:00:51,976
So then which view is right?

18
00:00:52,376 --> 00:00:56,376
Turns out there are numerous problems in making it on your own.

19
00:00:56,976 --> 00:01:02,176
You don't recognize the issues that are standing in your way, or if you do, you don't necessarily know how to fix them.

20
00:01:02,776 --> 00:01:07,376
And the result is that somewhere along the way, you stop improving.

21
00:01:09,524 --> 00:01:14,624
And I thought about that, and I realized that was exactly what had happened to me as a surgeon.

22
00:01:15,524 --> 00:01:25,224
So I asked a former professor of mine who had retired, his name is Bob Osteen, and he agreed to come to my operating room and observe me.

23
00:01:25,724 --> 00:01:28,724
The case, I remember that first case, it went beautifully.

24
00:01:28,924 --> 00:01:31,824
I didn't think there would be anything much he'd have to say when we were done.

25
00:01:32,224 --> 00:01:35,624
Instead, he had a whole page of dents with notes.

26
00:01:37,824 --> 00:01:39,124
Just small things, he said.

27
00:01:39,624 --> 00:01:43,824
Did you notice that the light had swung out of the wound during the case?

28
00:01:44,424 --> 00:01:48,824
Another thing I noticed, he said, your elbow goes up in the air every once in a while.

29
00:01:49,424 --> 00:01:51,724
That means you're not in full control.

30
00:01:52,124 --> 00:01:54,924
It was a whole other level of awareness.

31
00:01:55,824 --> 00:01:58,324
He was describing what great coaches do.

32
00:01:58,824 --> 00:02:04,124
And what they do is they are your external eyes and ears, providing a more accurate picture of your reality.

33
00:02:04,624 --> 00:02:06,524
They're recognizing the fundamentals.

34
00:02:07,124 --> 00:02:11,824
They're breaking your actions down and then helping you build them back up again.

35
00:02:12,424 --> 00:02:16,524
After two months of coaching, I felt myself getting better again.

36
00:02:17,224 --> 00:02:21,224
And after a year, I saw my complications drop down even further.

37
00:02:22,324 --> 00:02:23,124
It was painful.

38
00:02:24,124 --> 00:02:25,624
I didn't like being observed.

39
00:02:26,524 --> 00:02:29,424
And at times I didn't want to have to work on things.

40
00:02:29,524 --> 00:02:32,724
I also felt there were periods where I would get worse before I got better.

41
00:02:33,824 --> 00:02:38,524
But it made me realize that the coaches were onto something profoundly important.

42
00:02:38,924 --> 00:02:43,624
I think it's not just how good you are now, I think it's how good you're going to be that really matters.
